---- Changes since 5.2 ----
First version of Cloudmin GPL for KVM.
When adding a disk to a virtual system that uses LVM for storage, you can now select which volume group to create the disk in (or use a regular file).
Added the wait-for-system API command, for delaying until one or more systems are usable.
---- Changes since 5.3 ----
System owners can now change their UI language on the Change Password and Email page.
High resolution VGA graphics can now be enabled for new or editing KVM virtual systems.
Added the Roundrobin DNS Records page, for creating DNS entries that are automatically updated to point to only managed systems that are alive.
The SSH and Webmin passwords for a virtual system can now be viewed by clicking on the Show link on the Edit System page.
Added an option on the disk creation page to overwrite an existing file or LV, and an equivalent --overwrite flag to the create-disk command.
If the Cloudmin system cannot be a KVM host due to running the wrong kernel or missing KVM tools, a warning message is now shown after logging in.
---- Changes since 5.4 ----
The new auto-scaling groups feature allows virtual systems to be created and destroyed in response to CPU load or other factors. This can be used to scale up a service like a website or replicated database to handle higher traffic, by cloning and configuring new virtual systems automatically.
---- Changes since 5.5 ----
The Change Password page and passwd-system API command can now be used to set the VNC password for the graphical console of a system.
IPv6 addresses can now be managed on KVM instances and assigned at creation time. The IP Addresses page also allows entering of IPv6 allocation ranges for use by new systems.
New KVM instances can now have virtual memory added at creation time, using a separate virtual disk.
---- Changes since 5.6 ----
Added support for creating virtual disks with ext3 and ext4 filesystems.
The monitor for new and newly restarted KVM instances can now be accessed via a TCP port, which allows Cloudmin to perform some configuration changes without requiring a reboot.
Virtual disks and network interfaces can now be added to and removed from running KVM instances without a reboot, assuming they support PCI hot-plugging.
KVM virtual systems can now be paused and resumed, and live migrated to another host without requiring a shutdown.
---- Changes since 5.7 ----
When creating a KVM instance on a host system with multiple bridges, you can now select which bridges to automatically allocate IP addresses.
KVM systems with multiple disks now use a new backup format that stores each disk image in a separate file. This allows backups to be created and restored without consuming any temporary disk space on the host system.
The SSH port for new virtual systems can now be set on the Cloudmin Configuration page, and via the --ssh-port flag to the create-system API command.
Package updates are now installed via a single YUM or APT command, if the system being updated has Webmin 1.580 or Virtualmin 3.89 or later.
---- Changes since 5.8 ----
The DNS roundrobin page now has an option to limit the number of IP addresses to include in the resulting DNS entries, to allow the simple configuration of DNS-based failover. DNS roundrobins can also include an arbitrary list of IP addresses, which do not necessarily have to be Cloudmin managed systems.
Disks on virtual systems can now be excluded from backups on the Edit Disk page, and using the modify-disk API command. Swap disks are now also automatically excluded.
---- Changes since 5.9 ----
Proxy balancers can now also be configured on the DNS roundrobins page, if you have a system running Virtualmin. This allows a single website to be served by multiple backend systems, which get updated depending on their status.
Systems to backup can now be selected by group, owner or virtualization type, as wells as by hostname.
Additional directories for virtual system disk images can now be defined, and one selected when creating, cloning or moving a system. Access to directories can be limited by system owner plan, virtualization type and host system.
---- Changes since 6.0 ----
The Change Password page can now be used to set the password Cloudmin uses to login to a system, as well as changing the actual password. It has also been moved to the top of the left menu.
System alerts can now be triggered by the amount of incoming our outgoing network bytes / second on managed systems.
CPU usage for KVM instances can now be limited on host systems that support cgroups.
When editing a real network interface (like eth1) on a virtual system, the bridge it is connected to on the host system can now be changed.
Network interfaces on non-Linux virtual systems can now be managed, although their IP addresses must be configured inside the VM.
The master administrator can now define custom links that appear on the left menu for some or all virtual systems, for linking to site-specific applications or documentation.
---- Changes since 6.1 ----
KVM virtual systems can now have an IO class set, which controls the priority of their disk IO operations compared to other VMs on the same host.
The ISO file for a CD attached to a virtual system can now be changed while the system is running.
Backups now include the details of systems, as well as their disk images. This allows Cloudmin to re-create a deleted system from a backup as part of the restore process. In the UI, this must be done from the Backup Logs page.
---- Changes since 6.2 ----
When calling the remote API with the json, perl or xml format flags, multiline mode is automatically enabled so that the output from commands can be correctly parsed. API errors are also returned using the selected format.
---- Changes since 6.3 ----
Added a button to the Edit System page to kill any lock held on the system by other Cloudmin processes.
The SSH cipher can now be set on a per-system basis, on the Change Password page.
---- Changes since 6.4 ----
New packages can now be installed on multiple systems from the Package Updates page, or with the update-systems API command with the --install flag.
The number of cores for KVM systems can now be set, in addition to the number of virtual CPUs. This allows the creation of VMs that appear to have (for example) 4 cores but only 2 physical CPUs.
A minimum IO class for KVM and OpenVZ virtual systems can now be defined at the plan level, so that system owners cannot create or modify systems to have an excessive IO priority.
KVM systems can now be prevented from using IP or MAC addresses that have not been assigned to them, to prevent collision with other real or virtual systems.
When a backup is restored that contains disks whose size doesn't match the target system, disks are resized first to match the backup.
---- Changes since 6.5 ----
Systems to backup can now be selected by host or location group.
---- Changes since 6.6 ----
Additional command-line parameters for KVM instances can now be set when creating or editing a system.
The disk space available on a host system for a new virtual disk or system now takes the directory to be used into account.
Moved all background cron jobs (except existing backups) to Webmin's built-in scheduler, to save memory and reduce the CPU load of launching cron jobs.
---- Changes since 6.8 ----
When a new virtual system is created, its timezone is set to match the host system rather than using the zone in the image.
When resizing the root disk on a running system, the user is now prompted to have it shut down and re-started as part of the resize process.
Added a tab to the IP Addresses page to block IP addresses from being allocated to new systems.
---- Changes since 6.9 ----
Virtual systems can now be backed up to and restored from Amazon S3 and Rackspace Cloud Files.
Bandwidth data files are now split up into weekly chunks, which reduces the run time of the bandwidth.pl cron job and makes graphing bandwidth usage faster.
Virtual systems running FreeBSD can now be managed by Cloudmin in just the same way as Linux systems. This includes IP address assignment, disk creation and deletion, imaging and status monitoring.
---- Changes since 7.0 ----
Added a page and API commands for creating snapshots of virtual systems, and rolling their state back to those snapshots.
---- Changes since 7.1 ----
Virtual disks on KVM systems can now be moved to a different directory or LVM volume  group.
Usage by system owners now correctly takes deletion and re-assignment of systems into account.
Bandwidth usage by systems owned by a particular owner is now displayed on the Edit Owner page, and is correctly computed taking deletion and re-assignment into account.
---- Changes since 7.2 ----
The graphical console for virtual systems is now displayed using a Flash object by default, with the option to switch back to Java.
---- Changes since 7.3 ----
---- Changes since 7.4 ----
Added a Cloudmin Configuration option to limit the time for which system statistics and bandwidth usage data is kept.
Moved the page for importing existing image files to be accessible from a new link on the left menu, and added support for downloading pre-built Xen and KVM images from Stacklet.
The statistics graph page for a single system can now show multiple stats at the same time.
---- Changes since 7.5 ----
Post-creation scripts can be now be set on a per-image basis.
A single virtual system can now be backed up quickly, either to another system, and FTP server, for download in the browser.
Images only available to subscribers can now be downloaded from Stacklet.
Added the edit-file API command, for easily editing a single file on a remote server.
---- Changes since 7.6 ----
Added a file manager for browsing the filesystems of real and virtual systems, even those that are shut down.
System alerts can now be configured to send email when the alert condition stops firing.
---- Changes since 7.7 ----
Systems that require root access via sudo can now be managed by Cloudmin, and EC2 instances that require sudo are automatically detected at creation time.
CentOS 7 is now supported as host system and virtual system type, including the ability to manage XFS filesystems and network interfaces with the ip command.
---- Changes since 7.8 ----
Whether or not to start new virtual systems at host boot time can now be controlled on the system creation form, and via a Cloudmin Configuration page option.
System statistics for the fraction of disk, RAM and swap space used can now be graphed and alerted on.
Backups can now be restored on Cloudmin master systems other than the one they were made on, even if the orginal system image is missing or if the original host is no longer available.
New disks can now be created with GPT partition tables, if supported by the parted command.
---- Changes since 7.9 ----
Additional SSH keys for a system can now be viewed and selected on the Change Password page, set with the passwd-system API command, and displayed with the list-system-keys command.
Added the vnc-system API comment for opening up a port to the graphical console on a virtual system.
---- Changes since 8.0 ----
When creating an image that will be stored on the Cloudmin master, it is now compressed directly to the master system so that no temporary disk space is needed on the host.
When syncing domains between Virtualmin 1.750 or later systems, replication mode is now used to prevent over-writing of shared storage like MySQL databases or NFS-exported home directories.
---- Changes since 8.2 ----
When deleting a replicated Virtualmin domain, Cloudmin will preserve shared resources until the last replica is removed.
When creating a Virtualmin domain on a system with sycning enabled, Cloudmin can now replicate it immediately to other hosts.
Added the Running Actions page to view and terminate operations with locks on Cloudmin systems. Also created the equivalent list-system-locks API command.
---- Changes since 8.4 ----
Virtualmin backups can be scheduled across multiple Cloudmin-managed systems to a common destination.
---- Changes since 9.0 ----
The storage location for a Xen or KVM instance's disks can now be selected at VM creation time, rather than always using the host system default.
Added a button to the backup form to immediately execute a backup, but in the background.
